/**
 * @defgroup optimal_specific_speed_calculator Pump Specific Speed
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Calculates the US customary specific-speed value used in the pump-efficiency correction.
 * @details
 * What specific-speed value represents a pump duty after accounting for head per stage?
 * This calculation combines rotational speed, liquid flow and total head to provide the input
 * used by the separate specific-speed efficiency correction. It does not search for an optimum
 * rotational speed or establish the best-efficiency point of a particular pump.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Interpretation}
 * The result is specific speed in the US customary numerical convention:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-result; N_s=\frac{n\sqrt{Q}}{(H/s)^{3/4}}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{N_s; Returned US customary pump specific speed; \mathrm{rpm}\sqrt{\mathrm{US\ gpm}}/\mathrm{ft}^{3/4}}
 * @symrow{n; Pump rotational speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{Q; Supplied liquid volumetric flow; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{H; Total pump head across all stages; \foot}
 * @symrow{s; Supplied number of pump stages; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A result of about 2233.60 describes the combination of 2000 rpm, 2000 US gpm and 137 ft
 * of head for one stage. It is not a speed of 2233.60 rpm, an efficiency percentage or a
 * dimensionless speed. Higher rotational speed or flow increases the result at fixed head
 * per stage; greater head per stage decreases it. The efficiency correction also needs the
 * pump style, which is not an input to this calculation.
 *
 * @heading{Use the Required Unit Convention and Head per Stage}
 * Supply rotational speed in rpm, flow in US gallons per minute and total pump head in feet.
 * Convert other unit systems before applying the relationship. Using an SI flow or head number
 * without conversion changes the numerical result and makes it unsuitable for the separate
 * correction curves that expect this US customary convention.
 *
 * For a multistage pump, the calculation first interprets the total head through:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-stage-head; H_{stage}=\frac{H}{s}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{H_{stage}; Head per pump stage used in the specific-speed denominator; \foot}
 * @symrow{H; Supplied total pump head; \foot}
 * @symrow{s; Supplied number of pump stages; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * This division treats the head contribution of the stages equally. Do not supply an already
 * divided stage head together with the total stage count, which would divide the head again.
 * The flow is used exactly as supplied. No pump-style-specific adjustment, including halving
 * the flow for a double-suction pump, is performed. Assess that convention before comparing
 * this result with a value obtained using a different inlet-flow definition.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * Consider a three-stage pump at 1800 rpm, 1000 US gpm and 100 ft of total head. The head
 * per stage is approximately 33.3333 ft, so:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-example;
 *   N_s=\frac{1800\sqrt{1000}}{(100/3)^{3/4}}\approx4103.1127
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{N_s; Example US customary pump specific speed; \mathrm{rpm}\sqrt{\mathrm{US\ gpm}}/\mathrm{ft}^{3/4}}
 * @symrow{1800; Example pump rotational speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{1000; Example liquid flow; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{100; Example total pump head; \foot}
 * @symrow{3; Example stage count; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A one-stage pump with the same speed, flow and total head gives 1800 in the same convention.
 * The three-stage result is larger because the formula uses less head per stage. Carry the
 * unrounded result into the specific-speed correction and round only for presentation.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Arithmetic Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * Interpret the result for positive finite rotational speed, flow, head and stage count, with
 * a head-per-stage interpretation appropriate to the pump. The calculation accepts a floating
 * stage count, including fractional values, without rounding it to an integer. That arithmetic
 * capability does not establish that a fractional physical stage is meaningful.
 *
 * No validation, defaults, denominator guards or clamping are applied. For otherwise positive
 * finite inputs, zero flow returns zero, zero head returns positive infinity and zero stage
 * count returns zero through the unguarded arithmetic. Negative flow or a negative head-per-stage
 * ratio produces NaN. Negative rotational speed can produce a negative result. Signed zero,
 * nonfinite values, overflow and underflow propagate according to the formula; these outcomes
 * are not accepted operating duties or error codes.
 *
 * This relationship does not validate a manufacturer curve, determine pump suitability, or
 * supply an efficiency by itself. A specific-speed value calculated from an arbitrary operating
 * point does not establish a manufacturer's best-efficiency-point classification. The retained
 * calculation does not establish a validated operating range or uncertainty for the downstream
 * empirical efficiency correction. Use the same unit and flow conventions when comparing results.
 *
 * @see optimal_specific_speed::calculate
 */
